Lab 6: Automated Visual QA in DevOps
Objective
Integrate automated visual quality assurance (QA) using Selenium into a local CI/CD pipeline with Jenkins. This simulates automated visual testing during deployment — entirely offline.

Prerequisites
Start your Al Nafi-provided Ubuntu machine

Install the following:

Jenkins:
sudo apt update
sudo apt install jenkins -y
sudo systemctl start jenkins
sudo systemctl enable jenkins
Access Jenkins at: http://localhost:8080

Java (required by Jenkins):
sudo apt install default-jre -y
Python & Selenium:
pip install selenium
Install Chrome and Chromedriver:
sudo apt install chromium-browser chromium-chromedriver -y
Verify:

chromium-browser --version
chromedriver --version
Steps
Step 1: Create a Simple Visual QA Test with Selenium
Create a directory for the test:

mkdir -p ~/visual-qa-lab
cd ~/visual-qa-lab
Create a test script visual_check.py:

from selenium import webdriver
from selenium.webdriver.chrome.options import Options

options = Options()
options.add_argument('--headless')
options.add_argument('--no-sandbox')
options.add_argument('--disable-dev-shm-usage')

driver = webdriver.Chrome(options=options)
driver.get("https://example.com")  # Use any internal/test site here

title = driver.title
if "Example" in title:
    print("Page title check passed.")
else:
    print("Page title check failed!")

screenshot = "screenshot.png"
driver.save_screenshot(screenshot)
print(f"Screenshot saved as {screenshot}")

driver.quit()
Run and test locally:

python3 visual_check.py
Step 2: Set Up Jenkins to Run Visual QA Script
Open Jenkins at http://localhost:8080

Log in and create a Freestyle Project: Visual_QA_Pipeline

Under Build Steps, choose "Execute Shell", and add:

cd ~/visual-qa-lab
python3 visual_check.py
Save and build the project

Check console output and confirm:

Page title check passed

Screenshot was saved

Optional Enhancements
Use git to pull test code into Jenkins

Store test reports or screenshots in a specific folder

Extend the test to compare screenshots using Pillow or OpenCV for pixel difference

Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Built a local CI/CD pipeline with Jenkins

Integrated Selenium to perform visual checks on a web application

Captured and saved screenshots as part of automated QA

This offline lab simulates real-world DevOps pipelines where visual testing is essential for UI validation — fully executable on your Ubuntu-based development environment.
